32
00:02:14,781 --> 00:02:18,180
and causing $800 million in property damage.

33
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:25,180
damage. The experts at Underwriters

34
00:02:25,181 --> 00:02:28,420
Laboratories in Northbrook, Illinois, are helping to reduce

35
00:02:28,421 --> 00:02:33,820
that danger. In the name of consumer safety,

36
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:36,640
the renowned product testing organization has been creating

37
00:02:36,641 --> 00:02:39,540
havoc under controlled laboratory conditions since

38
00:02:39,541 --> 00:02:45,940
1894. Chances are you'll see its seal of approval on many of

39
00:02:45,941 --> 00:02:47,640
of the electrical devices you own.

40
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:54,840
The UL label on a product means that the manufacturer

41
00:02:54,841 --> 00:02:58,060
has submitted their product to an independent third party

42
00:02:58,061 --> 00:02:59,420
for safety testing.

43
00:02:59,421 --> 00:03:03,560
That UL mark will tell consumers that the fire hazards,

44
00:03:03,561 --> 00:03:05,900
electrical hazards, mechanical hazards

45
00:03:05,901 --> 00:03:08,060
have been addressed by very rigorous standards

46
00:03:08,061 --> 00:03:09,360
and very rigorous testing.

47
00:03:09,361 --> 00:03:16,360
Every year, UL tests nearly 75,000 products.

48
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:27,220
Among them are hundreds designed to function inside your walls.

49
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,520
The job of UL engineers is to determine at what point they may

50
00:03:33,521 --> 00:03:35,620
fail and create a safety hazard.

51
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:42,960
These robotic fingers are part of that effort,

52
00:03:42,961 --> 00:03:45,400
putting wall switches through their paces

53
00:03:45,401 --> 00:03:47,760
to see if they'll hold up over time.

54
00:03:47,761 --> 00:03:50,600
Testing a switch that's used inside a wall

55
00:03:50,601 --> 00:03:53,200
where you can't see it or get to it very easily

56
00:03:53,201 --> 00:03:54,800
is very important.

57
00:03:54,801 --> 00:03:57,120
We have to make sure the switch does what it's supposed to

58
00:03:57,121 --> 00:04:00,920
without causing a fire or any hazard inside your walls.

59
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:07,560
UL's tests extend to the wires and cables themselves.

60
00:04:07,561 --> 00:04:10,960
Inside your walls, they can spread a fire by acting much

61
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,700
like a wick in a candle.

62
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:17,300
To earn UL's seal of approval, samples like this phone

63
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:21,200
and Internet cable must convey a flame no farther than 5 feet,

64
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:26,120
or 150 centimeters, before it burns out.

65
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:28,820
We're already at 125 centimeters,

66
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:31,420
and the way it's burning, it doesn't look like it's

67
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:33,020
going to self-extinguish.

68
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:35,960
We can see the flames are starting to get to the 150

69
00:04:35,961 --> 00:04:38,460
to the 150-centimeter point right now,

70
00:04:38,461 --> 00:04:40,960
as soon as we get char at that point,

71
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:42,260
that constitutes a failure.

72
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:50,580
In another part of the facility,

73
00:04:50,581 --> 00:04:54,320
this 25-foot-long furnace measures any product's capacity

74
00:04:54,321 --> 00:04:58,180
to carry flames within our walls.

75
00:04:58,181 --> 00:05:03,120
It was invented in 1922 by UL engineer Albert Steiner.

76
00:05:05,960 --> 00:05:09,500
This is the Steiner Tunnel, and we use this tool to evaluate

77
00:05:09,501 --> 00:05:12,160
this flame spread and the smoke developed from a building

78
00:05:12,161 --> 00:05:15,700
material. Today's test is going to be using these wires and

