00:05:42,081 --> 00:05:46,800
four feet before burning out. The flame can go to five feet,

84
00:05:46,801 --> 00:05:51,240
and if it stops there, if it stops propagating, then your

85
00:05:51,241 --> 00:05:53,840
sample's okay. But as soon as it hits five and a half,

86
00:05:53,841 --> 00:06:01,120
it's a failure. The insulation inside

87
00:06:01,121 --> 00:06:04,620
is another potential conveyor for fire.

88
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:07,320
Insulation has to meet the same five-foot safety standard

89
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:08,320
that wire must.

90
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:16,160
There it goes.

91
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:21,280
We're down to about one foot now, two feet, two and a half,

92
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:24,480
three, three and a half.

93
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:27,480
That looks like that might be about it.

94
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:29,520
UL not only fire tests components

95
00:06:29,521 --> 00:06:36,520
inside our walls like this, but also the walls themselves.

96
00:06:36,521 --> 00:06:43,520
Wall material manufacturers from all over the world send

97
00:06:43,521 --> 00:06:47,520
contractors and technicians to build full-scale assemblies and

98
00:06:47,521 --> 00:06:52,520
